					<article
						class="post-14130 post type-post status-publish format-standard has-post-thumbnail category-blog tag-activities tag-jurassic-days entry"
						aria-label="Jurassic Days: Dino Statue Driving Tour" itemscope=""
						itemtype="https://schema.org/CreativeWork">
						<h1 class="entry-title" itemprop="headline">Jurassic Days: Dino Statue Driving Tour</h1>
						<div class="entry-content" itemprop="text">
							<style>
								.site-inner {
									margin: 110px auto 30px;
									max-width: 70% !important;
									clear: both;
									word-wrap: break-word;
								}
							</style>



							<div class="wp-block-cover"
								style="background-image:url(https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Alphabetosaurus.jpeg);background-position:59% 46%">
								<div
									class="wp-block-cover__inner-container is-layout-flow wp-block-cover-is-layout-flow">
									<p class="has-text-align-center has-large-font-size"></p>
								</div>
							</div>



							<div class="wp-block-kadence-rowlayout alignnone">
								<div id="kt-layout-id_f42520-17" class="kt-row-layout-inner kt-layout-id_f42520-17">
									<div
										class="kt-row-column-wrap kt-has-1-columns kt-gutter-default kt-v-gutter-default kt-row-valign-top kt-row-layout-equal kt-tab-layout-inherit kt-m-colapse-left-to-right kt-mobile-layout-row">
										<div class="wp-block-kadence-column inner-column-1 kadence-column_b53ac8-2b">
											<div class="kt-inside-inner-col">
												<p>Dinosaurs became extinct 65 million years ago, but Pittsburgh is
													still a city of dinosaurs.&nbsp; The giants still roam the city’s
													hills even outside of the museum.&nbsp; In fact, you might see
													dinosaurs in your own neighborhood!</p>



												<p>Carnegie Museum of Natural History released 100 dinosaur statues
													during DinoMite Days in the summer of 2003. Some of these dinosaurs
													have roamed far away, but others remain within the Pittsburgh
													area.&nbsp; These aren’t ordinary dinosaurs, they’re spectacularly
													colorful and creative!&nbsp; Designed by local artists and
													imaginative students, these dinosaurs are representative of the
													vibrant spirit of Pittsburgh and some of the ‘Burgh’s amazing local
													legends.</p>



												<p>To commemorate the city’s dinosaurs this summer, consider visiting a
													few on a Dino Statue Driving Tour, or hunt for the elusive dinosaur
													statues not listed here.&nbsp; The tour takes roughly two
													hours—timed with a stop for a photo at each dinosaur, but I’d
													recommend adding a snack break—and includes 15 dinosaurs. The
													DinoMite Days dinosaurs released in 2003 came in three standard
													shapes—Tyrannosaurus, Stegosaurus, and Torosaurus.&nbsp; The
													dinosaurs were auctioned as part of a fundraiser for several local
													charities, including Carnegie Museum of Natural History’s
													<em>Dinosaurs in Their Time</em> exhibition.&nbsp; They are
													currently privately owned and may be publicly displayed or kept
													indoors.&nbsp; The dinosaurs have even ended up as part of other
													museums including the John Heinz History Center.&nbsp; Some
													dedicated dinosaur hunters are trying to locate all 100
													dinosaurs!<br><br><strong>Note: </strong>While many of these
													dinosaurs are displayed as public art, <strong>some are owned by
														private individuals and should be approached with
														respect.&nbsp;</strong> Please, be courteous when taking
													photographs and ask permission when necessary.&nbsp; You may be
													required to wear a mask or participate in a health screening at some
													of these stops for public health safety precautions.
												</p>



												<iframe src="./Jurassic Days_ Dino Statue Driving Tour_files/embed.html"
													width="640" height="480" alt="google map embed"></iframe>



												<div class="wp-block-kadence-rowlayout alignnone">
													<div id="kt-layout-id_26e52d-cc"
														class="kt-row-layout-inner kt-layout-id_26e52d-cc">
														<div
															class="kt-row-column-wrap kt-has-2-columns kt-gutter-default kt-v-gutter-default kt-row-valign-top kt-row-layout-right-golden kt-tab-layout-inherit kt-m-colapse-left-to-right kt-mobile-layout-row">
															<div
																class="wp-block-kadence-column inner-column-1 kadence-column_842e65-e1">
																<div class="kt-inside-inner-col">
																	<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img
																			decoding="async" width="768" height="1024"
																			src="./Jurassic Days_ Dino Statue Driving Tour_files/Fredosaurus-Rex-Friday-XIII.jpeg"
																			alt="Aaron with Fredosaurus Rex Friday XIII"
																			class="wp-image-14137"
																			srcset="https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Fredosaurus-Rex-Friday-XIII.jpeg 768w, https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Fredosaurus-Rex-Friday-XIII-225x300.jpeg 225w"
																			sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 768px">
																	</figure>
																</div>
															</div>



															<div
																class="wp-block-kadence-column inner-column-2 kadence-column_c9d2e1-97">
																<div class="kt-inside-inner-col">
																	<p><strong>First Stop:</strong> Fredosaurus Rex
																		Friday XIII<br><strong>Coordinates:</strong>
																		40.429910, -79.975938</p>



																	<p>This king of the dinosaurs is dedicated to the
																		friendliest neighbor, Mr. Rogers, and is
																		conveniently located at the WQED headquarters on
																		the South Side.&nbsp; Another Mr. Rogers’
																		Neighborhood dino lives at the Fred Rogers
																		Center in Latrobe, PA.</p>



																	<p></p>
																</div>
															</div>
														</div>
													</div>
												</div>



												<div class="wp-block-kadence-rowlayout alignnone">
													<div id="kt-layout-id_4e609f-ed"
														class="kt-row-layout-inner kt-layout-id_4e609f-ed">
														<div
															class="kt-row-column-wrap kt-has-3-columns kt-gutter-default kt-v-gutter-default kt-row-valign-top kt-row-layout-equal kt-tab-layout-inherit kt-m-colapse-left-to-right kt-mobile-layout-row">
															<div
																class="wp-block-kadence-column inner-column-1 kadence-column_4ef315-a1">
																<div class="kt-inside-inner-col">
																	<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img
																			loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="768"
																			height="640"
																			src="./Jurassic Days_ Dino Statue Driving Tour_files/Mr.-Dig-e1594062035854.jpeg"
																			alt="Aaron with Mr. Dig"
																			class="wp-image-14138"
																			srcset="https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Mr.-Dig-e1594062035854.jpeg 768w, https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Mr.-Dig-e1594062035854-300x250.jpeg 300w, https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Mr.-Dig-e1594062035854-600x500.jpeg 600w"
																			sizes="auto, (max-width: 768px) 100vw, 768px">
																	</figure>
																</div>
															</div>



															<div
																class="wp-block-kadence-column inner-column-2 kadence-column_b4affc-e7">
																<div class="kt-inside-inner-col">
																	<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img
																			loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024"
																			height="768"
																			src="./Jurassic Days_ Dino Statue Driving Tour_files/Ketchupsaurus.jpeg"
																			alt="Ketchupsaurus" class="wp-image-14139"
																			srcset="https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Ketchupsaurus.jpeg 1024w, https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Ketchupsaurus-300x225.jpeg 300w, https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Ketchupsaurus-768x576.jpeg 768w"
																			sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px">
																	</figure>
																</div>
															</div>



															<div
																class="wp-block-kadence-column inner-column-3 kadence-column_de16b0-dd">
																<div class="kt-inside-inner-col">
																	<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img
																			loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024"
																			height="768"
																			src="./Jurassic Days_ Dino Statue Driving Tour_files/Philliposaurus.jpeg"
																			alt="Philliposaurus" class="wp-image-14140"
																			srcset="https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Philliposaurus.jpeg 1024w, https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Philliposaurus-300x225.jpeg 300w, https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Philliposaurus-768x576.jpeg 768w"
																			sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px">
																	</figure>
																</div>
															</div>
														</div>
													</div>
												</div>



												<p><strong>Second stop:</strong> PPG Plaza Dino
													Parade<br><strong>Coordinates:</strong> 40.439766, -80.003858</p>



												<p>The second stop has all three dinosaur models!&nbsp; This dino parade
													is in PPG Plaza and includes Mr. Dig (T. Rex), Ketchupsaurus
													(Torosaurus), and Philliposaurus (Stegosaurus).&nbsp; Philliposaurus
													is named for Phillip Johnson, one of the architects of the PPG Place
													building.</p>



												<div class="wp-block-kadence-rowlayout alignnone">
													<div id="kt-layout-id_32b16f-33"
														class="kt-row-layout-inner kt-layout-id_32b16f-33">
														<div
															class="kt-row-column-wrap kt-has-2-columns kt-gutter-default kt-v-gutter-default kt-row-valign-top kt-row-layout-equal kt-tab-layout-inherit kt-m-colapse-left-to-right kt-mobile-layout-row">
															<div
																class="wp-block-kadence-column inner-column-1 kadence-column_1cd440-b5">
																<div class="kt-inside-inner-col">
																	<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img
																			loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024"
																			height="768"
																			src="./Jurassic Days_ Dino Statue Driving Tour_files/DNAsaurus.jpeg"
																			alt="DNAsaurus" class="wp-image-14148"
																			srcset="https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/DNAsaurus.jpeg 1024w, https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/DNAsaurus-300x225.jpeg 300w, https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/DNAsaurus-768x576.jpeg 768w"
																			sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px">
																	</figure>
																</div>
															</div>



															<div
																class="wp-block-kadence-column inner-column-2 kadence-column_3780c5-91">
																<div class="kt-inside-inner-col">
																	<p><strong>Third Stop</strong>:
																		DNAsaurus<br><strong>Coordinates:</strong>
																		40.446468, -80.018495</p>



																	<p><br>Our friends at the Carnegie Science Center
																		host DNAsaurus, which is appropriately decorated
																		with DNA strands and chromosomes. Bingo! Dino
																		DNA!</p>
																</div>
															</div>
														</div>
													</div>
												</div>



												<div class="wp-block-cover"
													style="background-image:url(https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Prehistoric-Printasaurus.jpeg);background-position:31% 62%;min-height:500px;aspect-ratio:unset;">
													<div
														class="wp-block-cover__inner-container is-layout-flow wp-block-cover-is-layout-flow">
														<p class="has-text-align-center has-large-font-size"></p>
													</div>
												</div>



												<p><strong>Fourth Stop:</strong> Prehistoric
													Printasaurus<br><strong>Coordinates:</strong> 40.460458, -79.980679
												</p>



												<p>Located on Herrs Island, Printasaurus is a collage of patterns and
													geometric shapes that make one stylin’ dino.&nbsp; You can park at
													Herrs Island North Park and take the Three Rivers Heritage Trail to
													find this dinosaur placed in a garden at the opposite end of the
													island.</p>



												<div class="wp-block-kadence-rowlayout alignnone">
													<div id="kt-layout-id_529ce5-b2"
														class="kt-row-layout-inner kt-layout-id_529ce5-b2">
														<div
															class="kt-row-column-wrap kt-has-2-columns kt-gutter-default kt-v-gutter-default kt-row-valign-top kt-row-layout-equal kt-tab-layout-inherit kt-m-colapse-left-to-right kt-mobile-layout-row">
															<div
																class="wp-block-kadence-column inner-column-1 kadence-column_f40d74-47">
																<div class="kt-inside-inner-col">
																	<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img
																			loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="768"
																			height="1024"
																			src="./Jurassic Days_ Dino Statue Driving Tour_files/Connections.jpeg"
																			alt="Connections dinosaur statue"
																			class="wp-image-14150"
																			srcset="https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Connections.jpeg 768w, https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Connections-225x300.jpeg 225w"
																			sizes="auto, (max-width: 768px) 100vw, 768px">
																	</figure>
																</div>
															</div>



															<div
																class="wp-block-kadence-column inner-column-2 kadence-column_9d9ae3-2f">
																<div class="kt-inside-inner-col">
																	<p><strong>Fifth Stop:
																		</strong>Connections<br><strong>Coordinates:</strong>
																		40.466769, -79.952309</p>



																	<p>Children’s Hospital has a few dinosaurs, but
																		Connections the T. Rex is visible from the
																		outside.&nbsp; This dinosaur celebrates the
																		interconnectedness of all life.</p>
																</div>
															</div>
														</div>
													</div>
												</div>



												<div class="wp-block-cover"
													style="background-image:url(https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Skeletosaurus-Formerly-Cogitatio-Aeterna.jpeg);background-position:47% 12%">
													<div
														class="wp-block-cover__inner-container is-layout-flow wp-block-cover-is-layout-flow">
														<p class="has-text-align-center has-large-font-size"></p>
													</div>
												</div>



												<p><strong>Sixth Stop:</strong> Formerly Cogitatio
													Aeterna<br><strong>Coordinates:</strong> 40.458010, -79.930777</p>



												<p>This remodeled skeletal dinosaur is appropriately located at the
													Greater Pittsburgh Orthopaedic Associates building on Centre Avenue.
												</p>
											</div>
										</div>
									</div>
								</div>
							</div>



							<div class="wp-block-kadence-rowlayout alignnone">
								<div id="kt-layout-id_fad9ed-a2" class="kt-row-layout-inner kt-layout-id_fad9ed-a2">
									<div
										class="kt-row-column-wrap kt-has-2-columns kt-gutter-default kt-v-gutter-default kt-row-valign-top kt-row-layout-equal kt-tab-layout-inherit kt-m-colapse-left-to-right kt-mobile-layout-row">
										<div class="wp-block-kadence-column inner-column-1 kadence-column_d6a92f-00">
											<div class="kt-inside-inner-col">
												<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy"
														decoding="async" width="1024" height="768"
														src="./Jurassic Days_ Dino Statue Driving Tour_files/Bill-Dollarsaurus.jpeg"
														alt="Bill Dollarsaurus" class="wp-image-14153"
														srcset="https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Bill-Dollarsaurus.jpeg 1024w, https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Bill-Dollarsaurus-300x225.jpeg 300w, https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Bill-Dollarsaurus-768x576.jpeg 768w"
														sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px"></figure>
											</div>
										</div>



										<div class="wp-block-kadence-column inner-column-2 kadence-column_5055f3-a6">
											<div class="kt-inside-inner-col">
												<p><strong>Seventh Stop:</strong> Bill
													Dollarsaurus<br><strong>Coordinates:</strong> 40.447218, -79.894962
												</p>



												<p>On the corner at the Shady Lane School you can find Bill
													Dollarsaurus.&nbsp; Bill has a sister named Dolly who lives
													somewhere in the city.</p>
											</div>
										</div>
									</div>
								</div>
							</div>



