										class="kt-row-column-wrap kt-has-2-columns kt-gutter-default kt-v-gutter-default kt-row-valign-top kt-row-layout-equal kt-tab-layout-inherit kt-m-colapse-left-to-right kt-mobile-layout-row">
										<div class="wp-block-kadence-column inner-column-1 kadence-column_de118c-62">
											<div class="kt-inside-inner-col">
												<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy"
														decoding="async" width="1024" height="768"
														src="./Jurassic Days_ Dino Statue Driving Tour_files/Alphabetosaurus-1.jpeg"
														alt="Alphabetosaurus" class="wp-image-14159"
														srcset="https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Alphabetosaurus-1.jpeg 1024w, https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Alphabetosaurus-1-300x225.jpeg 300w, https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Alphabetosaurus-1-768x576.jpeg 768w"
														sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px"></figure>
											</div>
										</div>



										<div class="wp-block-kadence-column inner-column-2 kadence-column_b6f2d0-01">
											<div class="kt-inside-inner-col">
												<figure class="wp-block-image size-large is-resized"><img loading="lazy"
														decoding="async"
														src="./Jurassic Days_ Dino Statue Driving Tour_files/Dino-soar.jpeg"
														alt="Dino-soar" class="wp-image-14160" width="384" height="512"
														srcset="https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Dino-soar.jpeg 768w, https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Dino-soar-225x300.jpeg 225w"
														sizes="auto, (max-width: 384px) 100vw, 384px"></figure>
											</div>
										</div>
									</div>
								</div>
							</div>



							<p><strong>Last Stop!</strong><br><strong>Coordinates: </strong>40.443159, -79.949601</p>



							<p>The final three dinosaurs on our tour happen to reside near the Carnegie Museum of
								Natural History (where else would dinosaurs be?).&nbsp; Alphabetosaurus is across Forbes
								Avenue from the museum.&nbsp; Dino-soar is inside of the Portal Entry to the museum and
								features many beautiful birds including Archaeopteryx, one of the first birds.</p>



							<p>And our very last stop is Dippy the Diplodocus.&nbsp; Though not a part of DinoMite Days,
								Dippy is a very special life-size model of a Diplodocus and is certainly an inspiring
								dinosaur statue.&nbsp; Although all black, Dippy occasionally wears scarves, ties, and
								hats to fit in with the changing attitude of Pittsburgh.&nbsp; Right now, Dippy is
								wearing a mask to help keep everyone safe.</p>



							<p>Make sure to tag the Carnegie Museum of Natural History in photos you post to social
								media!</p>



							<div class="wp-block-cover"
								style="background-image:url(https://carnegiemnh.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Dippy.jpeg);background-position:73% 48%">
								<div
									class="wp-block-cover__inner-container is-layout-flow wp-block-cover-is-layout-flow">
									<p class="has-text-align-center has-large-font-size"></p>
								</div>
							</div>



							<p>Written by: Aaron Young, Museum Educator</p>
